Now I understand the vulnerability. The `ih264d_parse_sei_message` checks `CHECK_BITS_SUFFICIENT(ps_bitstrm, (ui4_payload_size << 3))` which ensures there are at least `payload_size * 8` bits available. However, `ih264d_parse_fgc` reads bits based on the values in the payload (like `au1_num_intensity_intervals_minus1` and `au1_num_model_values_minus1`), which can cause it to read far more bits than the payload size allows. This means it can read past the end of the bitstream buffer.

The key issue is in the nested loops in `ih264d_parse_fgc`:
```c
for(i = 0; i <= ps_sei->s_sei_fgc_params.au1_num_intensity_intervals_minus1[c]; i++)
{
    // reads 8 + 8 bits
    for(j = 0; j <= ps_sei->s_sei_fgc_params.au1_num_model_values_minus1[c]; j++)
    {
        // reads sev (variable length)
    }
}
```

If `au1_num_intensity_intervals_minus1` is set to 255 and `au1_num_model_values_minus1` is set to 7, the function will try to read a huge number of bits, far exceeding the payload size. This causes the `GETBITS` macro to read past the end of the buffer.

